      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Libertarians ground their opposition to taxation not merely on inefficiency but on a deontological premise that all coercive appropriation of property without owner consent constitutes a moral violation akin to theft or murder. For this framework to be rigorous, it requires a non-ideal theory that accepts state legitimacy as derived solely from the inherent right of individuals to self-ownership and the acquisition of property through voluntary exchange. Consequently, the state's existence in many libertarian models is limited to a private defense agency, raising the sophisticated philosophical challenge of defining a \"minima state\" as no larger than a necessary mutual defense enclave against aggression. This view posits that even the collective obligation argument fails because the coercive nature of taxation inherently infringes upon the very autonomy that any individual owes to themselves.",
